course: dictionary
lesson: 12024
title: Short, chatty words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are shortened to sound friendly and quick. «Mum bought a box of chocs for the party.» Chocs is a short, chatty way of saying chocolates, and you hear it in shops and adverts. Read the words around it: a box of chocs means a box of sweets, so the meaning is clear.
explain: Shortened words are called informal, because we use them when we talk to family and friends. «Dad ate a choc ice on the beach.» A choc ice is an ice cream bar covered in chocolate. The word choc is still the clue, because it tells you the chocolate is the important part.
explain: Choc is used most in the UK, Australia and New Zealand. «I put choc chips in my cookies.» Choc chips are small pieces of chocolate, so the words around choc help you see what it means. Try your meaning in the sentence: small pieces of chocolate in cookies makes sense, so you have worked it out.
fact: Choc is a short, informal word for chocolate.
fact: A choc can mean one chocolate sweet, or a box of chocs can mean many.
fact: Choc is used in compound words such as choc ice, choc chip and choc-top.
fact: A choc-top is an ice-cream cone dipped in chocolate, and you hear this word mostly in Australia and New Zealand.
fact: The old jokey phrase «Chocs away!» means let's go.
exercise: «Mum bought a box of chocs for the party.» What is a choc? | A type of bread | A small toy car | *Chocolate, or a single chocolate sweet
exercise: «Dad ate a choc ice on the beach.» What is a choc ice? | A cold drink with ice cubes | *An ice cream bar covered in chocolate | A sandcastle tower
exercise: Which word is the short, chatty form of chocolate? | *choc | chock | chill | chip
test: Dad said, «Let's grab a choc-top before the film.» What is a choc-top? | A bag of crisps | *An ice-cream cone dipped in chocolate | A chocolate cake with cream
test: Ella said, «Can I have a choc chip cookie?» What are choc chips? | *Small pieces of chocolate | Dried cherries | Sugar crystals
exam: The old jokey phrase «Chocs away!» means what? | Eat your dinner | Stop right now | *Let's go | We have lost
